description:
## Bug

In `decode_line` (decode.rs), when the escape character `=` is immediately followed by `\r` (0x0D) or `\n` (0x0A), the escape branch fires and decodes the newline byte as a data byte instead of treating `=` as a lone/split escape.

## Root cause

`decode_line` receives the raw line bytes INCLUDING the trailing `\r\n` from `LineIter`. The escape branch (line 224) checks `i + 1 < len` and, if true, reads the next byte unconditionally — including `\r` or `\n`. The lone-`=` branch (line 231) only fires when `=` is the absolute last byte of the slice, which does not cover `=\r` or `=\n`.

## Impact

Input line `b"*=\r\n"` produces decoded bytes `[0, 163]` instead of `[0]`. Input `b"*=\n"` produces `[0, 160]` instead of `[0]`.

This affects any encoder that naively wraps the encoded byte stream at a fixed column width without ensuring escape pairs are not split — the `=` lands at column 127 (the last data column before CRLF) and the escaped byte lands at the start of the next line. The current decoder would silently produce a garbage byte (163 or 160) and consume the CRLF as part of the escape, emitting one wrong decoded byte per such occurrence.

The Python fixture generator (`gen_fixtures.py`) uses this split-unsafe wrapping strategy (`yenc_line_wrap` slices encoded bytes at `line_len` with no escape-pair awareness). No existing fixture triggers this because the test payload `bytes(range(64))` happens to have its only escape pair well inside the 128-byte line. A payload with an escape pair straddling position 127/128 would produce a corrupt decode.

## Minimal repro (Rust)

```rust
let mut out = Vec::new();
// '*' = byte 0; then '=' should be lone escape before \r\n
yencoding::decode(b"=ybegin line=128 size=1 name=f\n*=\r\n=yend size=2\n");
// decode_line on b"*=\r\n" gives [0, 163], not [0]
```

## Fix

In the `=` escape branch of `decode_line`, skip past any leading `\r`/`\n`/`\0` bytes when seeking the escape target, or check that the next byte is not a framing byte before consuming it:

```rust
b'=' if i + 1 < len => {
    let next = line[i + 1];
    if matches!(next, b'\r' | b'\n' | b'\0') {
        // Lone '=' before line-end framing — discard the '=' and let
        // the framing bytes be handled normally on the next iteration.
        i += 1;
    } else {
        out.push(next.wrapping_sub(106));
        i += 2;
    }
}
```

## Test to add

```rust
#[test]
fn decode_line_eq_before_crlf_is_lone_escape() {
    // '=' immediately before \r\n must NOT consume the \r as an escape target.
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    decode_line(b"*=\r\n", &mut out);
    assert_eq!(out, &[0]); // only '*' decoded; lone '=' discarded
}
```
